<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  WinSW configuration for the BGE Reranker v2-m3 HTTP service.

  Naming rule (WinSW requirement): the XML file name MUST match the
  executable base name. The exe is "CowReranker.exe", so the config is
  "CowReranker.xml" — WinSW discovers it by convention, not by argument.

  SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH
  The service does NOT carry its own copy of the startup parameters.
  It runs cmd.exe with start-reranker.bat in foreground mode, which is
  exactly what a person double-clicking that file runs. The model
  directory, port and thread count live in reranker.conf and are read by
  serve.py.

  Why this matters: the model directory used to be written down here AND
  in the batch launcher AND in serve.py's defaults. Changing one left the
  others pointing at the old path. The symptom was a service that
  started, reported "healthy", and then failed every request with a
  model load error. One place to change = that failure mode is gone.

  Note: XML comments may not contain a double hyphen, so the foreground
  switch is referenced by name only in the comments below.
-->
<service>
  <id>CowReranker</id>
  <name>CowAgent BGE Reranker</name>
  <description>BGE-reranker-v2-m3 HTTP service (port 9898) for CowAgent memory retrieval reranking.</description>

  <!--
    Run the batch launcher through cmd.exe, not python directly.

    cmd.exe is the executable: WinSW monitors THAT process, and cmd.exe
    stays in the foreground for as long as the server runs. Launching
    python directly would work too, but then the service and the manual
    start are two different code paths again, and they drift.

    The batch file is invoked with the foreground switch so that it runs
    the server IN THIS PROCESS instead of detaching it. The detached form
    returns immediately, which would make the service report "stopped"
    while the server is still alive and holding port 9898.

    Paths are absolute on purpose. A Windows service runs under
    LocalSystem with a minimal environment: no user PATH, no USERPROFILE
    pointing at the interactive account, and a different working
    directory. Relative paths and PATH lookups both fail there.
  -->
  <executable>C:\Windows\System32\cmd.exe</executable>
  <arguments>/c "C:\Workspace\AI-Agent\CowAgent\docker\reranker\start-reranker.bat" --foreground</arguments>
  <workingdirectory>C:\Workspace\AI-Agent\CowAgent\docker\reranker</workingdirectory>

  <!--
    The batch launcher locates itself via %~dp0 and reads its parameters
    from reranker.conf, so no parameter is duplicated here. The only
    thing this config still owns is the SERVICE environment, which is
    about the service account rather than the application.

    A service runs under LocalSystem: USERPROFILE points at
    C:\Windows\System32\config\systemprofile, and HOME is not set at all.
    Without these, transformers may try to reach huggingface.co at
    startup (a hang on a machine with no outbound access) or fail
    writing a cache into a directory it does not own.

    Offline mode: the model is a local directory, so the hub is never
    needed.
  -->
  <env name="HF_HUB_OFFLINE" value="1" />
  <env name="TRANSFORMERS_OFFLINE" value="1" />
  <env name="HF_HOME" value="C:\Users\User\cow\reranker-cache\hf" />
  <env name="TORCH_HOME" value="C:\Users\User\cow\reranker-cache\torch" />
  <env name="TEMP" value="C:\Users\User\cow\reranker-cache\tmp" />
  <env name="TMP" value="C:\Users\User\cow\reranker-cache\tmp" />

  <!--
    Start mode: Automatic + delayedAutoStart. Delayed start means this
    service starts AFTER the network stack and other auto-start services,
    which keeps a 2.27GB model load from competing with the user logon.
  -->
  <startmode>Automatic</startmode>
  <delayedAutoStart />

  <!--
    Restart on failure. uvicorn exits non-zero on an unrecoverable error;
    without this the service would stay dead until someone noticed, and
    CowAgent would silently degrade to un-reranked results.

    IMPORTANT: this only works if the batch launcher reports a NON-ZERO
    exit code when the server dies. WinSW 2.12.0's WrapperService.cs
    does this with the child's exit code:

        if (this.orderlyShutdown) { ...log only... }
        else {
            // by not reporting unclean shutdown, we let Windows SCM to
            // decide if it wants to restart the service automatically
            if (proc.ExitCode == 0) { this.SignalStopped(); }
            Environment.Exit(proc.ExitCode);
        }

    Exit code 0 -> WinSW explicitly reports SERVICE_STOPPED -> the SCM
    treats it as a clean stop and never applies the failure actions, so
    the service stays down. The launcher therefore normalises a zero
    exit code to 1 in foreground mode.

    A deliberate "sc stop" is unaffected: DoStop() sets the
    orderlyShutdown flag, and that branch never looks at the exit code.
  -->
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="10 sec" />
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="30 sec" />
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="60 sec" />
  <resetfailure>1 hour</resetfailure>

  <!--
    Graceful shutdown: uvicorn handles SIGTERM and drains in-flight
    requests. WinSW sends Ctrl+C first, then escalates to TerminateProcess
    after stoptimeout. 15s is enough for a single CPU-only inference.
  -->
  <stoptimeout>15 sec</stoptimeout>
  <stopparentprocessfirst>true</stopparentprocessfirst>

  <!-- LocalSystem by default; explicit for clarity and for auditing. -->
  <serviceaccount>
    <user>LocalSystem</user>
  </serviceaccount>

  <priority>Normal</priority>

  <!--
    Logs: roll-by-size so the service cannot fill the disk over months.
    keepFiles=5 at 10MB each = 50MB ceiling per log stream.
  -->
  <logpath>C:\Users\User\cow\reranker-logs</logpath>
  <log mode="roll-by-size">
    <sizeThreshold>10240</sizeThreshold>
    <keepFiles>5</keepFiles>
  </log>
</service>
